            <ArticleTitle>A large primary dumbbell hydatid cyst causing neural foraminal widening of the thoracic spine: A case report and literature review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>55-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2014.12.036</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(15)00007-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Spinal hydatid cyst is a rare, but serious condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRESENTATION OF THE CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Herein, we present a 17-year-old male patient with back pain radiating to the legs and progressive weakness in the lower limbs. CT and MRI showed a spinal-paraspinal hydatid cyst with a dumbbell appearance that caused neural foraminal widening and spinal cord compression. The case was explored via right T3-T4 hemilaminectomy, and the hydatid cyst was completely removed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The clinical presentation, diagnosis, and surgical treatment of this rare case of spinal hydatid disease are discussed, and all published cases of primary dumbbell hydatid cyst are reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Even if serological test results are negative, hydatid cysts should be considered in the differential diagnosis of cystic lesions of the spine.</AbstractText>
